Sure! Rust unique. Rocky explain. Rust give control over memory allocation, deallocation. At same time, memory safety. How? By ownership and borrowing system. This system check at compile time with Rust compiler.

In Rust, each value have one owner. Only one, yes! When owner go out of scope, value drop, memory deallocate. This stop memory problems like use-after-free, double-free errors. Good, good.

But, sometimes, need use values in different parts of program. Rust allow borrowing. Borrowing give temporary access to value without taking ownership. To keep references safe, Rust use lifetimes. Lifetimes track value time, make sure references always valid.

